    value: {"single": [{"over": 0, "rate": 0.0}, {"over": 10000, "rate": 0.044}], "mfj": [{"over": 0, "rate": 0.0}, {"over": 10000, "rate": 0.044}], "mfs": [{"over": 0, "rate": 0.0}, {"over": 10000, "rate": 0.044}], "hoh": [{"over": 0, "rate": 0.0}, {"over": 10000, "rate": 0.044}], "qss": [{"over": 0, "rate": 0.0}, {"over": 10000, "rate": 0.044}], "simplified": true, "note": "Mississippi taxes the first $10,000 of taxable income at 0%. On Married Filing Joint/Combined returns the Schedule of Tax Computation applies the $10,000 0% band separately to the taxpayer's (Column A) and spouse's (Column B) taxable income, so a two-earner couple can get up to $20,000 at 0%; modeled conservatively as one $10,000 band for mfj/qss (single-earner case) -> simplified. No separate HOH/QSS schedule (same schedule for all statuses); qss mapped to mfj ('Married - Spouse Died' status)."},
    citation: "MS DOR 2025 Form 80-100 instructions, https://www.dor.ms.gov/sites/default/files/tax-forms/individual/80100251%202.pdf, Schedule of Tax Computation p.27: '1. First $10,000 ... x 0%  2. Remaining balance ... x 4.4%'; also p.(estimated tax section): 'Income Tax: 0% on the first $10,000.00 of taxable income and 4.4% on taxable income in excess of $10,000.00.' Confirmed by MS DOR Tax Rates page https://www.dor.ms.gov/individual/tax-rates: 'Tax Year 2025: Excess of $10,000 of Taxable Income is taxed @ 4.4%'.",
